<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tripolitania Vilayet</h1><p class="st-meta">1835 CE – 1912 CE · capital Tripoli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Libya under direct Ottoman rule from 1835, the last Ottoman province in Africa until the Italian invasion of 1911.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1835 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, fearing French intervention after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, ended the rule of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karamanli-tripoli">Karamanli Tripoli</a> and restored direct administration. Ottoman forces spent the following decades subduing tribal revolts, notably that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghuma-al-mahmudi">Ghuma al-Mahmudi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Jabal Nafusa</a>, which lasted until 1858.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The province, organised as a vilayet under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> reforms, included <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-fezzan">Fezzan</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> was later administered separately. Governors introduced new courts, schools and conscription, but authority in the interior was limited, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/senussi-order">Senussi Order</a> dominated Cyrenaica's hinterland. The trans-Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> persisted until late in the century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> invaded in 1911, and after war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ouchy">Treaty of Ouchy</a> in 1912 transferred the province to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-libya">Italian Libya</a>. Local resistance, supported by Ottoman officers, continued for years.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tripolitania-vilayet.jpg" alt="Tripolitania Vilayet" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Bamse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maritime_flag_of_Regency_of_Tripoli_(18th_century).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1835 CE – 1912 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripoli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Ottoman Turkish, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ottoman Libya, Vilayet of Tripoli</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=tripolitania-vilayet" aria-label="Tripolitania Vilayet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 983 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tripolitania Vilayet lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 1239L5338 1239M5338 1239L5709 970M5338 1239L5338 1239M5338 1239L5638 1347"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0M5709 970h0M5338 1239h0M5638 1347h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tripoli. <a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=tripolitania-vilayet">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
